+{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T18:05:00Z","note":"Target: for positive integers a,b, if k=(a^2+b^2)/(ab+1) is integral, prove k square. Shared GM and fact graph initially empty. Direct descent: choose a counterexample minimizing a+b and order a>=b. Equation a^2+b^2=k(ab+1). Let a'=kb-a, so aa'=b^2-k and substitution gives a'^2+b^2=k(a'b+1). If b^2<k, then a-kb=(k-b^2)/a is a positive integer, hence k-b^2=a(a-kb)>=a; but a>kb>=k, contradicting k-b^2<k. Thus b^2>=k. Equality gives k=b^2 square; strict inequality gives 0<a'=(b^2-k)/a<b^2/a<=b, so (a',b) is a smaller counterexample. This should verify as a complete self-contained proof."}

